  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cameroon - Floods</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The intervention is critical due to extensive damage from flooding that has destroyed livelihoods, homes, infrastructure, and increased health risks, including potential outbreaks of cholera and malaria. The affected populations are highly vulnerable and need immediate assistance to recover and stabilize their living conditions.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The operation aims to support 99,100 people affected by the severe floods in Cameroon, particularly in the Far North and West regions. Key areas of intervention include multi-purpose cash assistance, health services, water, sanitation, and hygiene (WASH), as well as protection, gender inclusion, and community engagement.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The operation aims to support 99,100 people affected by the severe floods in Cameroon. Assistance targets the most affected and vulnerable groups, including families who have lost homes, women- or child-headed households, and people with specific needs. Vulnerable populations in temporary shelters, as well as communities at risk of further escalation, are also prioritized.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The primary focus is on the Far North and West regions, with additional monitoring and preparedness for the Northwest and Southwest regions if flood risks intensify.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The strategy includes distributing cash to the most affected households, training volunteers for on-the-ground support, enhancing community-based surveillance for health risks, and setting up water purification and hygiene promotion efforts. The operation emphasizes local capacity-building, with the Cameroon Red Cross Society leading on-ground activities, backed by international support from IFRC, ICRC, and the French Red Cross.</narrative>
  </description>
